From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: renaud <rlpirou@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 06:11:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] BTC-CORE hooks: parallel world ticking

With parallel-world-ticking.enabled the world part of tickChildren goes
through dev.btc.core.world.WorldTickLoop: each world ticks on its own
thread, the main thread waits for all of them, then carries on. The
vanilla loop is left as it is and stays the path taken when the option is
off (the default).

- tickChildren: one branch in front of the loop; the world tick events
  (WorldTickStartEvent / WorldTickEndEvent) are fired around level.tick in
  both paths, and only when something listens.
- moonrise$executeMidTickTasks: on a world thread, the chunk tasks of that
  world only, with a back-off kept by the thread instead of the two
  server-wide fields. One hunk covers every mid-tick call site.
- removeLevel: the world's tick thread stops with it.
- stopServer: every world thread stops.

Derived from DivineMC, patch "Parallel world ticking" (NONPLAYT, BX Team),
GPL-3.0, https://github.com/BX-Team/DivineMC - itself derived from
SparklyPaper (SparklyPower), https://github.com/SparklyPower/SparklyPaper.
The tick events are SparklyPaper's idea, rewritten.

Differences: the executors and the permits live in WorldTickCoordinator
(no field added to ServerLevel or DedicatedServer); the barrier waits for
every world and reports every failure where DivineMC rethrows the first;
mid-tick tasks keep their call sites and their rhythm where DivineMC
switches them off and polls once per tick.

